<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
=============================================================================
 CardDemo migration  |  services/common-lib/pom.xml
 Maven module descriptor for the shared kernel of the migrated services.
=============================================================================

 PURPOSE
 Declares the `common-lib` Maven module: the shared kernel that every
 migrated CardDemo service compiles against. It is the Java analogue of
 compiling every COBOL program with one copybook include path. The
 reference test suite states that convention explicitly, building each
 program with `cobc ... -I app/cpy` and requiring that a record layout is
 never duplicated but kept single sourced from `app/cpy/`
 [tests/README.md section 5.2 L268; section 12 L541 to L542]. Migration
 rule T2 carries the same discipline into Java: one COBOL `COPY` becomes
 one type import, and the shared concerns (money, the fixed width, zoned
 decimal and packed decimal codecs, the error model, the correlation
 filter, the keyset page envelope, the validation flags) live ONLY here so
 that no service re-declares them.

 The concerns this module owns, and therefore the dependencies it needs,
 come straight from the immutable COBOL baseline:

   * Money is zoned decimal with sign overpunch, for example
     `ACCT-CURR-BAL PIC S9(10)V99` [app/cpy/CVACT01Y.cpy:L7]. The reference
     suite records that reading such a field with the compiler's default
     ASCII sign convention silently corrupts negative balances, which is
     why `-fsign=EBCDIC` is mandatory there [tests/README.md L273 to L274].
     Java therefore carries money as `BigDecimal` at scale 2, never as
     `float` or `double`.

   * Packed decimal appears alongside it: the export layout declares
     `EXPORT-SEQUENCE-NUM PIC 9(9) COMP` and
     `EXP-ACCT-CURR-BAL PIC S9(10)V99 COMP-3`
     [app/cpy/CVEXPORT.cpy:L16, L50], and the authorization segments use it
     pervasively. Two distinct numeric codecs are therefore required, not
     one.

   * Arithmetic order is part of the contract. The interest calculation
     multiplies before it divides:
     `COMPUTE WS-MONTHLY-INT = ( TRAN-CAT-BAL * DIS-INT-RATE) / 1200`
     [app/cbl/CBACT04C.cbl:L464 to L465]. Dividing first yields different
     cents on many inputs, so the shared money helper preserves the order
     rather than leaving it to each caller.

   * The abend work area [app/cpy/CSMSG02Y.cpy] is the source of the
     structured error detail this module exposes to all eight services.

 POSITION IN THE REACTOR
 FIRST module built. All eight service modules depend on `common-lib`;
 `common-lib` depends on NONE of them, so this descriptor declares ZERO
 intra reactor dependencies. The dependency arrow points inward only.

 THIS MODULE IS A LIBRARY, NOT A DEPLOYABLE
 It has no Spring Boot application class, no `application.yml`, no
 database migration directory, no OpenAPI contract, no actuator endpoint,
 no Dockerfile and no container registry repository of its own. The
 repository wide counts are therefore deliberately asymmetric and must not
 be "corrected" into agreement: 9 Maven modules, 8 Dockerfiles, 10
 container images (the 8 services plus `ui` plus `data-migration`).
 Reconciling the module count with the image count would provision an
 eleventh phantom registry repository for a module that publishes no image.

 PACKAGE ROOT
 Fixed and non-negotiable: `com.carddemo.common`. Every type in this module
 lives beneath it, which is what lets the architectural test forbid cross
 service imports by package name alone.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 PARAMETERS (the inputs this descriptor supplies to the build)

   parent        `com.carddemo:carddemo-services:1.0.0-SNAPSHOT`, resolved
                 from `../pom.xml`. Supplies the Java 21 toolchain, every
                 dependency version and the inherited documentation gate.
   artifactId    `common-lib`, matching this directory name exactly.
   packaging     `jar`, an ordinary classpath jar.
   dependencies  Four Spring Boot starters marked optional, plus Jackson 3
                 databind (group `tools.jackson.core`, the generation Boot 4
                 builds its default mapper from) and Micrometer core as
                 exported API, plus two test scoped artifacts.

 RETURNS
   Two artifacts. `com.carddemo:common-lib:1.0.0-SNAPSHOT`, written as
   `target/common-lib-1.0.0-SNAPSHOT.jar`: a plain jar whose classes sit at
   the archive root and are therefore directly usable as a classpath entry
   by the eight dependent service modules. And the test artifact of the same
   coordinate at type `test-jar`, written as
   `target/common-lib-1.0.0-SNAPSHOT-tests.jar`, carrying the architecture
   package alone: it is how the eight services receive the one layering rule
   class, and the `build` element below explains why it is produced before
   the `package` phase rather than during it. Test reports stay at their
   default `target/surefire-reports/` location, which is where the services
   pipeline collects them.

 EXCEPTIONS (how this descriptor makes a build fail, by design)
   * A documentation violation at severity warning or above fails the
     `validate` phase, because the inherited Checkstyle gate is configured
     to fail rather than warn. That gate is the machine checkable half of
     this project's Explainability rule.
   * A missing or unresolvable parent fails before any goal runs, since
     every version used here is inherited rather than declared.
   * The layering rules, authored in this module's architecture test package
     under the simple name `LayeringRulesTest`, fail the `test` phase when an
     architectural boundary is crossed, using the ArchUnit engine this
     descriptor puts on the test classpath. That simple name is the one the
     `architecture-rules` include pattern in `services/pom.xml` selects, so the
     name is a contract rather than a local choice.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 Assumptions: every dependency version is owned by `services/pom.xml` and,
 transitively, by the Spring Boot 4.1.0 parent that it inherits from. This
 file declares group id, artifact id and scope ONLY. The single `<version>`
 element in the whole file is the one inside `<parent>`. Re-pinning a
 version here would silently diverge this module from the other eight,
 which is precisely the failure the single copybook include path exists to
 prevent on the COBOL side.

 Assumptions: XML 1.0 section 2.5 forbids two consecutive hyphens anywhere
 inside a comment, so this file cannot use the house em dash idiom that the
 Java, Python and shell sources use, and cannot rule a comment off with
 hyphens. Rules here are drawn with `=` and `~`, line ranges are written as
 "L464 to L465", and long command line options are described in words
 rather than quoted. One stray pair of hyphens in any comment below would
 make this descriptor unparseable and the build would not start at all.
=============================================================================
-->
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">

  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <!--
   Assumptions: this module inherits every dependency version, every plugin
   version and every plugin binding from the aggregator, which in turn
   inherits them from the Spring Boot 4.1.0 parent. `<relativePath>` is
   stated explicitly so the reactor reads the aggregator from the working
   tree; left to its default, a stale aggregator previously installed into
   the local repository could satisfy the reference instead, and the module
   would then build against versions nobody can see in the source.

   Trade-offs: `<groupId>` and `<version>` are deliberately NOT repeated on
   this module even though restating them would make its full coordinates
   readable without opening the parent. Inheriting them keeps exactly one
   place to edit at release time; restating them creates two, and a module
   whose coordinates have drifted from its reactor is the usual result.
  -->
  <parent>
    <groupId>com.carddemo</groupId>
    <artifactId>carddemo-services</artifactId>
    <version>1.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    <relativePath>../pom.xml</relativePath>
  </parent>

  <!--
   Assumptions: the aggregator lists this module first in its module block,
   and Maven resolves a module entry to a directory and then reads the
   artifact id from the descriptor inside it. The directory name and the
   artifact id must therefore agree character for character; if they do not,
   the reactor does not resolve this module at all and the failure surfaces
   as the eight services being unable to find their shared kernel rather
   than as anything that names this file.
  -->
  <artifactId>common-lib</artifactId>

  <name>CardDemo Shared Kernel</name>
  <description>
    Shared kernel for the migrated CardDemo services: fixed point money,
    zoned decimal and packed decimal codecs, the fixed width record codec,
    the CSV authorization codec, the error and abend model, the correlation
    id filter, the keyset page envelope, the JWT role converter, the metric
    tag configuration, the timestamp formatter and the date edit validator.
    Consumed by all eight service modules; consumes none of them.
  </description>

  <!--
   Alternatives Considered: `pom` packaging, rejected because this module
   compiles real Java and must publish a jar of classes rather than
   aggregate other modules; and `war`, rejected because nothing here is
   deployed into a servlet container. `jar` is stated explicitly rather than
   left to Maven's default so the contract this module offers its eight
   consumers, an ordinary classpath jar, is legible without the reader
   having to know what the default is.
  -->
  <packaging>jar</packaging>

  <dependencies>

    <!--
     The four starters below carry types that this module's own sources
     implement against, so they must sit on ITS compile classpath:
     `web` for the servlet filter in `CorrelationIdFilter` and the
     controller advice in `GlobalExceptionHandler`; `validation` for the
     Jakarta constraint annotations used by `FieldValidationFlag` and the
     shared error types; `oauth2-resource-server` for the JWT and
     authentication converter types `JwtRoleConverter` is built on; and
     `security` for the granted authority values that converter produces.

     Alternatives Considered: `provided` scope, rejected because it asserts
     that a surrounding runtime container supplies the artifact, which is
     untrue here. A Spring Boot service packages its own server, so
     `provided` would document a deployment model this project does not
     have. Plain `compile` scope was rejected for a concrete reason rather
     than a stylistic one: it would push a servlet container transitively
     onto EVERY consumer of this module, including any consumer that is not
     a web application, and would undercut the boundary the aggregator
     draws deliberately by carrying no web starter in its own dependency
     block. Marking them optional keeps them on this module's compile
     classpath and stops them at its boundary.

     Trade-offs: optional dependencies are not inherited, so each of the
     eight consuming services must declare for itself whichever starters it
     uses; nothing arrives implicitly. That repetition is accepted because
     it keeps every service's dependency list an honest description of what
     that service is, instead of a list that grows quietly because a shared
     library happened to need a web stack. It is safe for a second reason
     too: `LayeringRulesTest` constrains web types only inside `..domain..`
     packages, and this module has no `domain` package at all, so holding
     web types here crosses no boundary.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
      <optional>true</optional>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-validation</artifactId>
      <optional>true</optional>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-security</artifactId>
      <optional>true</optional>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-oauth2-resource-server</artifactId>
      <optional>true</optional>
    </dependency>

    <!--
     `RethrowingDigestErrorHandler`, in the one package
     `com.carddemo.common.messaging.listener`, implements the queue starter's
     own error handler interface and takes a Spring messaging Message, so the
     starter's listener artifact must sit on THIS module's compile classpath.
     The library artifact is named rather than the starter, because this module
     needs the two types only and not the auto configuration a starter brings.

     WHY the handler is shared at all: the pinned starter's error handler stage
     RECOVERS from a failure, so a handler that returns normally leaves the
     pipeline result successful and the message is DELETED rather than
     redelivered. Three per service copies of a control with that property is
     three chances for one to be edited into swallowing a failure; one copy is
     negative tested once.

     Alternatives Considered: plain `compile` scope, rejected because it would
     push an SQS client transitively onto the four services that own no queue
     at all, which is the same boundary the four starters above are optional to
     preserve. It differs from the `ssm` dependency further down, which is
     deliberately NOT optional: forgetting that one would leave a fail closed
     write gate silently absent, whereas forgetting this one is a COMPILATION
     failure in the consuming service, since that service's own listener
     configuration is already written against the same starter types. A loud
     failure needs no defensive non optional declaration.

     Assumptions: four service modules declare
     io.awspring.cloud:spring-cloud-aws-starter-sqs today, and those four are
     the ones that can register this handler. No `<version>` is declared: the
     aggregator imports io.awspring.cloud:spring-cloud-aws-dependencies in its
     `dependencyManagement`, so the version resolves from that single pin.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.awspring.cloud</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-cloud-aws-sqs</artifactId>
      <optional>true</optional>
    </dependency>

    <!--
     `OnlineWriteGate` reads the one Parameter Store entry the nightly batch
     state machine toggles around its write window, so this module needs a
     Systems Manager client on its compile classpath. Until this dependency
     was added, this module carried NO AWS SDK at all, and that absence is
     what the gate's design had to be reconciled with rather than worked
     around.

     Alternatives Considered, and why each was rejected:

       * Injecting the flag's VALUE as an environment variable, which needs
         no SDK. Rejected because the value would then be frozen at task
         start: a task that started while the window was closed would keep
         refusing writes until it was replaced, and a task that started
         before a quiesce would keep accepting them straight through it. The
         gate has to be able to observe a CHANGE, which means reading at
         request time, which means a live client. This is also why the task
         definition carries the parameter's NAME and not its value.
       * `spring-cloud-aws-starter-parameter-store`, which the aggregator
         already version manages. Rejected because that starter's purpose is
         to import parameters into the Spring Environment during context
         refresh, which is the same freeze-at-start behaviour in a different
         place, and it would additionally pull a configuration-import
         mechanism into a library whose consumers configure themselves.
       * Holding the gate in each service instead, so that only the gated
         services carry the SDK. Rejected because the fail-closed decision
         would then exist in seven copies, and seven copies of a safety
         control is seven chances for one of them to be subtly fail-open.

     Assumptions: no `<version>` is declared, deliberately. The aggregator
     imports `software.amazon.awssdk:bom` in its `dependencyManagement`, so
     the version resolves from that single pin and this module cannot drift
     away from the version every other module resolves.

     Trade-offs: this dependency is NOT optional, unlike the four starters
     above, and the reason is a safety one rather than a convenience one. If
     it were optional, a service that is meant to be gated would have to
     remember to declare it; forgetting would leave the gate's classes absent
     and the gate itself either crashing the context or silently not applying
     — and a silently absent write gate is precisely the fail-open outcome the
     gate exists to prevent. Making it non-optional means the classes are
     always present and the ONLY thing that decides whether the gate is
     active is the `carddemo.control.online-writes.parameter-name` property,
     which the infrastructure injects into exactly the online workloads. The
     cost is that a consumer with no write window to respect carries an
     unused client; that is a small artifact against a control that cannot be
     accidentally omitted.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>software.amazon.awssdk</groupId>
      <artifactId>ssm</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
     `MoneyModule` is a Jackson module: it is what makes money serialise as
     a JSON string, so that no client parses an exact fixed point amount
     into an IEEE 754 double and loses cents at the boundary the user
     actually sees. The module type it implements is part of this module's
     exported API, because every consumer registers it, so unlike the four
     starters above this dependency is deliberately NOT optional. It has to
     reach consumers.

     Assumptions: the coordinate is the Jackson 3.x line, group
     `tools.jackson.core`, and the group id is the load bearing part of it.
     The Spring Boot 4.1.0 parent imports TWO Jackson bills of material, one
     for the 2.x line under `com.fasterxml.jackson` and one for the 3.x line
     under `tools.jackson`, so BOTH lines are version managed and both can
     end up on one classpath at once. Only one of them is the line Boot 4's
     HTTP stack actually uses: the web starter above resolves the 3.x
     mapper, and a module registered against it must implement
     `tools.jackson.databind.JacksonModule`.

     Refactoring Rationale: this coordinate previously named
     `com.fasterxml.jackson.core:jackson-databind`, the 2.x line. That
     resolved, compiled and shipped, and it silently did nothing: a Jackson
     2 `com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.Module` cannot be registered with a
     Jackson 3 `ObjectMapper`, because the two lines share no type. The
     effect was that money serialised through Boot's own default handling
     instead — as a bare JSON NUMBER — which is exactly the outcome
     `MoneyModule` exists to prevent, and the only symptom was a client
     losing cents on a value large enough to exceed a double's exact integer
     range. Nothing in a build or a startup log reports it, so the wrong
     group id is recorded here rather than left to be rediscovered.

     Trade-offs: the version is inherited from the managed 3.x bill of
     material rather than stated here. That gives up the ability to hold this
     one artifact at a version of its own, and it buys the guarantee that the
     mapper this module contributes to and the module it contributes are the
     same major line, which is the failure this coordinate has already had
     once.

     Assumptions: mapper generations are not mixed anywhere in this tree.
     Boot 4 leaves `spring.jackson.use-jackson2-defaults` off, every service
     configures the 3.x property shapes under `spring.jackson`, and no module
     declares a 2.x DATABIND coordinate; a single generation is what lets the
     module bean this library exports be picked up by the one mapper the
     framework builds. Declaring the 2.x databind artifact alongside this one
     would put two mapper implementations on a single classpath and
     reintroduce the ambiguity about which of them serves a response.

     Assumptions: `com.fasterxml.jackson.core:jackson-annotations` still
     appears on this module's resolved compile classpath, arriving as a
     transitive of the coordinate above, and its presence is correct rather
     than the mixing this comment forbids. Jackson 3 deliberately kept the
     ANNOTATIONS artifact on the older group id so that a type annotated for
     2.x is still annotated for 3.x; only databind and core moved. A reader
     auditing the classpath for a stray 2.x artifact needs that distinction,
     because the annotations jar is the one 2.x coordinate that is expected
     to be there.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>tools.jackson.core</groupId>
      <artifactId>jackson-databind</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
     `MetricsConfig` contributes a meter registry customizer that stamps the
     common service, environment and version tags onto every metric, so the
     registry types are needed at compile time.

     Alternatives Considered: declaring a concrete registry implementation
     here, so that all eight services exported metrics without each having
     to opt in. Rejected because the export format is a per service
     deployment decision, and binding it in the shared kernel would take
     that decision away from all eight at once while adding an exporter to
     the classpath of any consumer that does not scrape at all. Only the
     vendor neutral core is shared; each service chooses its own registry.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.micrometer</groupId>
      <artifactId>micrometer-core</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
     `spring-boot-starter-opentelemetry` supplies the OpenTelemetry SDK,
     Micrometer tracing bridge and OTLP exporter that every service uses to send
     spans to the collector sidecar in its ECS task.

     Refactoring Rationale: each service already exposes Prometheus metrics, but
     no dependency previously created or exported a trace. An infrastructure
     collector cannot recover spans an application never emits, so the tracing
     bridge belongs on the shared runtime classpath while the AWS-specific
     exporter destination remains task configuration.

     Assumptions: this dependency is deliberately NOT optional. All eight
     services depend on common-lib, and the tracing contract is cross-cutting;
     making it optional would require eight duplicate declarations and would let
     one bounded context silently disappear from a distributed trace. Spring
     Boot 4.1.0 manages the starter and its OpenTelemetry versions, so no
     independent version is declared here.

     Trade-offs: the shared kernel now carries one runtime integration rather
     than only vendor-neutral tracing types. The alternative was the Java agent,
     which would require every service image to download and copy a second
     executable artifact. The starter keeps the same application JAR runnable
     locally because carddemo-common-defaults.yml disables OTLP export by
     default, while ECS activates it explicitly through environment variables.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-opentelemetry</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
     Assumptions: `CorrelationIdFilter` writes the correlation id into the
     SLF4J mapped diagnostic context, and the SLF4J API arrives transitively
     through the logging starter that sits inside every starter declared
     above, so it is deliberately not declared on its own. Declaring it
     directly would create a second, independently resolved path to the
     logging API; when two such paths disagree the binding chosen at startup
     is effectively arbitrary, and the symptom is silent loss of log output
     rather than a build error. Letting the starter own it keeps exactly one
     path.
    -->

    <!--
     Assumptions: every test class in this module ends in `Test`, so Surefire
     runs them all in the `test` phase and no Failsafe execution or include
     pattern override is needed. That naming is the whole of the selection
     contract, which is why it is stated here rather than configured. This
     module has no integration test and needs none: it touches no database,
     no queue and no container, which is a
     direct consequence of it owning no schema and no transport. Report
     output stays at the default location the services pipeline collects
     from; relocating it would silently empty the pipeline's test report
     step while every test still passed locally.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-test</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
     Assumptions: `LayeringRulesTest`, under
     `src/test/java/com/carddemo/common/architecture/`, is the single owner
     of the architectural invariants for the entire reactor, so the ArchUnit
     engine has to resolve on THIS module's test classpath. Its version,
     1.4.2, is managed by `services/pom.xml` and must not be re-pinned here.
     The services pipeline may invoke that class by name as a labelled step
     of its own, so the class name and its path are a fixed external
     contract, not an implementation detail that can be renamed freely.

     Assumptions: single ownership is NOT single execution, and the two were
     previously conflated here. Maven publishes this module's main classes to
     its eight consumers and never its test classes, so declaring the engine
     and the rule class in one place delivers them nowhere on its own. The
     `build` element below packages that one class as this module's test
     artifact, each service declares that artifact at test scope, and the
     `architecture-rules` Surefire execution in `services/pom.xml` runs it
     inside each service against that service's own compiled classes. All
     three are required; any one of them alone leaves the rules authored and
     unenforced, which is indistinguishable from not having written them.

     Assumptions: "inherited by all eight service modules" is a statement
     about a MECHANISM, and the mechanism is named here so nobody has to
     infer it. Maven inheritance moves configuration from a parent to a
     child; it never moves a dependency's test classes onto a consumer's
     test classpath, and this module is a dependency of the eight services
     rather than their parent. The inheritance is therefore carried by three
     cooperating declarations: the `test-jar` execution in this file's build
     section publishes these test classes as a `tests`-classified artifact,
     each service declares that artifact at test scope, and
     `services/pom.xml` configures Surefire's `dependenciesToScan` so the
     runner looks inside it for test classes instead of only inside the
     module being built. Remove any one of the three and the gate silently
     stops running in all eight services while every build stays green,
     which is the precise failure this note exists to prevent.

     Assumptions: the ArchUnit engine does NOT reach the eight services
     through this declaration, because `test` scope is not transitive. Each
     service declares `archunit-junit5` itself, at test scope, for the same
     reason: a discovered `LayeringRulesTest` needs the engine on the
     classpath of the module that runs it, and without it the class fails to
     load rather than failing an assertion.

     Alternatives Considered: expressing the same package boundaries through
     Checkstyle's `ImportControl` module, which is already available given
     the inherited gate and would need no new dependency. It is deliberately
     absent from the shared Checkstyle configuration and must not be added
     here or anywhere else, because two enforcement points for one invariant
     drift apart, and the moment they disagree it is whichever one is
     cheaper to silence that gets silenced. Layering therefore has exactly
     one owner, and that owner is an executable test, so the rule cannot rot
     into a comment that no longer matches the code.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.tngtech.archunit</groupId>
      <artifactId>archunit-junit5</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
     TEST SCOPE ONLY. `RethrowingDigestErrorHandlerTest` asserts the one
     framework contract the handler's whole registration strategy rests on:
     that the queue starter's auto configuration still takes an error handler
     FROM THE CONTEXT and installs it on the listener container factory it
     builds. Three services publish that handler as a bean and none of them
     touches the factory, so if that parameter were ever withdrawn upstream the
     bean would simply stop being installed, the framework's own full throwable
     record would return in its place, and nothing anywhere would fail.

     Alternatives Considered: asserting the same contract inside one of the
     three consuming services, each of which already has this artifact through
     the starter. Rejected because the contract belongs to the shared handler
     rather than to any one service, and putting it in one of the three would
     leave the other two depending on a guard that lives somewhere arbitrary
     and could be deleted with that service.

     Assumptions: `test` scope is not transitive, so this reaches no consumer,
     and the test jar this module publishes carries only the layering rules
     into the services because Surefire's scan is filtered to that one simple
     name. No `<version>` is declared: the aggregator imports
     io.awspring.cloud:spring-cloud-aws-dependencies, which manages this
     artifact id, so it resolves from the same single pin as the sqs artifact
     above.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.awspring.cloud</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-cloud-aws-autoconfigure</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

  </dependencies>

  <!--
=============================================================================
 BUILD
 Exactly two plugin declarations, and both exist for one purpose: to hand
 this module's single architecture rule class to the eight service modules,
 and to keep it from being run twice here.
=============================================================================
  -->
  <build>
    <plugins>

      <!--
       Refactoring Rationale: the descriptors in this reactor previously
       stated that the eight service modules INHERITED the layering rules
       from this module. They did not and could not. Maven resolution passes
       a module's main classes to its consumers and never its test classes,
       so the rule class was compiled once, evaluated against this module
       alone, and never saw a single service class; the ArchUnit engine each
       service declared supplied an assertion API and no rules. Publishing
       the class as an artifact is what turns the single owner into a single
       owner that is actually executed everywhere.

       Assumptions: the goal is bound to `process-test-classes` rather than
       to its own default phase, `package`, and the binding is load bearing.
       Surefire's dependency scanner reads only files whose name ends in
       .jar; when a reactor dependency has not yet been packaged Maven
       satisfies it from `target/test-classes`, a DIRECTORY, which that
       scanner ignores in silence. Under the default binding a plain
       `mvn test` at the reactor root would therefore run no architecture
       rule at all while reporting success, because this module never reaches
       `package` in that lifecycle. Bound to `process-test-classes` the
       artifact is a real file, attached to this project, before any
       downstream module reaches its own `test` phase, so the gate behaves
       identically under `mvn test` and under `mvn verify`.

       Trade-offs: `includes` narrows the artifact to the architecture
       package instead of publishing every test class in this module. The
       compromise is that a consumer cannot reuse this module's codec
       fixtures or its money vectors, which is intentional: those suites
       belong to this module and re-running them once per service would
       report one defect eight times. What it buys is an artifact whose whole
       content is the shared contract, so nothing else can start travelling
       with it by accident. The package holds a documented
       `package-info.java`, so the archive is never empty.

       Assumptions: no version is stated, because `services/pom.xml`
       inherits the Spring Boot 4.1.0 parent's management of this plugin, and
       no `archive` configuration is stated either, so the test artifact is
       built with the same manifest settings as the main one.
      -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-jar-plugin</artifactId>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <id>architecture-rules-test-jar</id>
            <phase>process-test-classes</phase>
            <goals>
              <goal>test-jar</goal>
            </goals>
            <configuration>
              <includes>
                <include>com/carddemo/common/architecture/**</include>
              </includes>
            </configuration>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>

      <!--
       Assumptions: that execution exists in `services/pom.xml` to scan the
       test artifact above into a module that would otherwise never see the
       rule class. This module is where the class lives, so its own ordinary
       test execution already runs it; leaving the inherited execution active
       here would run it a second time in the same phase and overwrite the
       first result's report file with an identical one. The execution
       identifier MUST match the inherited one character for character,
       because Maven merges a module execution into an inherited execution
       only when the identifiers agree, and any other identifier would add a
       second execution instead of refining the first.

       Trade-offs: skipping is declared here rather than by making the
       inherited execution conditional on something. A profile or a property
       would put the decision at a distance from the module it concerns, and a
       reader of this file would have no way to tell that the rules still run
       here. The accepted cost is one plugin block whose only content is a
       skip, which is why this comment states what still runs.
      -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <id>architecture-rules</id>
            <configuration>
              <skip>true</skip>
            </configuration>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>

    </plugins>
  </build>

